From 847404b278691fc1281286abf9398ab5a4a19583 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mrinohk <5554998+Mrinohk@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 12:11:49 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 22/31] cuda : persistent full-attention layer halves on sm_60,
 faster multi-token reductions

Full-attention halves of the Qwen3.5/3.6 MoE layers (every 4th layer) for 1..6 tokens, under GGML_CUDA_P60_MEGA=1
(GGML_CUDA_P60_ATTN=0 disables them), as three launches instead of ~17 kernels:
- a persistent pre kernel: attn_norm, the q (+gate) / k / v projections as one stream of 9216 q8_0 rows, q / k RMS
  norm, M-RoPE (ggml's rope_multi angles, ext_factor 0), the f16 k / v cache rows;
- the multi-token flash-attention kernel (now also for 1 token, and at least one tile per KV range);
- a persistent post kernel: merge of the KV ranges, sigmoid gate, the output projection in two K halves, residual.
The matcher checks every node of the half, the rope parameters (shared by q and k, M-RoPE/IMRoPE, no offset, no
frequency factors) and that flash attention reads the cache rows the half writes.

The shared multi-token helpers move to p60-mt.cuh. Warp sums of the T token accumulators use one butterfly that
halves the vector per level (9 shuffles for 5..8 tokens instead of 5 per token) with one writer lane per token,
in every multi-token kernel; the output layer at 6 tokens: 1686 -> 1529 us.

2x P100, Qwen3.6-35B-A3B UD-Q4_K_XL:
  MTP n5 + draft vocab, prod sampler, 8 prompts x 256: 166.0 -> 171.0 t/s
  MTP at a 28k-token prompt:                            ~141 -> ~146 t/s
  no speculation, llama-bench tg128:                    116.8 -> 120.2 t/s
Wikitext KLD vs the stock ub=1 base: ub=6 0.00426 (same top token 97.77%, stock 97.53%), ub=1 0.00436 (97.63%).

Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5
